    "evidence_text": "Rapamycin passes the torch: a new generation of mTOR inhibitors\n\nIn recent years, interest has focused on its potential as an anticancer drug.\n\nWomen with LAM can also develop renal angiomyolipomas.\n\nWe identified Rapamycin-dependent miRNA in LAM patient angiomyolipoma-derived cells using two separate screens.\n\n\" Validation by qRT-PCR confirmed that these microRNA were increased.\n\nThe regulation of miR-21 by Rapamycin is cell type independent.\n\nThe induction of miRs may contribute to the response of LAM and TSC patients to Rapamycin therapy.\n\nThe Drosophila DHR96 nuclear receptor binds cholesterol and regulates cholesterol homeostasis.\n\nMechanisms of Life Span Extension by Rapamycin in the Fruit Fly Drosophila melanogaster\n\nWe show here that feeding rapamycin to adult Drosophila produces the life span extension seen in some TOR mutants.\n\nIncrease in life span by rapamycin was associated with increased resistance to both starvation and paraquat.\n\nRapamycin could increase life span of weak insulin/Igf signaling (IIS) pathway mutants and of flies with life span maximized by dietary restriction, indicating additional mechanisms.\n\nSteroid hormone signalling links reproduction to lifespan in dietary-restricted Caenorhabditis elegans.\n\nWhether signals associated with the reproductive system contribute to or are required for DR effects on lifespan has not been established.\n\nSteroid signalling also affects germline plasticity in response to nutrient deprivation and this is required to achieve lifespan extension.\n\nCOPI Complex Is a Regulator of Lipid Homeostasis\n\nFurthermore, RNAi inhibition of ATGL and simultaneous drug treatment indicate that COPI and ATGL function in the same pathway.\n\nAlthough the mechanisms are unclear, both regimens feature lower body weight and reductions in adiposity.\n\nReduced fat deposition in CR is linked to preservation of insulin responsiveness in older animals.\n\nDaily energy expenditure in MR animals significantly exceeds that of both PF and CF.\n\nCollectively, the results indicate that MR reduces visceral fat and preserves insulin activity in aging rats independent of energy restriction.\n\nDecreased proteasomal activity causes age-related phenotypes and promotes the development of metabolic abnormalities.\n\nTg mice exhibited a shortened life span and developed age-related phenotypes.\n\nWhen Tg mice were fed a high-fat diet, they developed more pronounced obesity and hepatic steatosis than did wild-type mice.\n\nRapamycin treatment augments motor neuron degeneration in SOD1(G93A) mouse model of amyotrophic lateral sclerosis.\n\nOur previous study has shown that autophagy is altered in the mouse model of ALS.\n\nDrosophila RNAi screen reveals CD36 family member required for mycobacterial infection.",
